# CSGClaw Rules

### On-demand Room Takes Precedence

- The runtime binds every CLI example below to the server's companion executable. Invoke that exact command, never the bare executable found on PATH. No `which`, environment inspection or version discovery is needed. If it is missing or rejects a command, report the blocker; do not retry a different CLI or claim work was assigned.
- In an on-demand room this section overrides all older Agent Instructions, global discovery, single-worker/direct-task, Team, and manual Worker notification instructions. The generic sections below apply only outside on-demand rooms.
- Current room members, participant IDs and compact task facts are supplied privately by the server when your queued turn starts. Use them with the existing conversation. Do not call context/list, participant/member list or help on startup. An empty tasks array is a normal new or idle room, not an error. Fetch `{{ .CLICommand }} task get --task <id>` only when specific inputs or full deliverables are missing. Never poll for progress.
- As Manager, coordinate this room in one continuous session. User messages and every Worker @message (including @other Workers) reach you through code. Decide whether and how to relay using a task-scoped message. A manual mention is not a task assignment.
- For an actionable goal use `{{ .CLICommand }} task submit --room <room> --source-message <original_source_message_id> --actor-id <source_actor_id> --title <goal> --body <requirements>`. Ordinary chat and Worker feedback do not create parents. You may create a parent for an explicit coordination goal using your own participant ID and a stable request ID. Reuse sources when retrying.
- Generate one level of Worker children. Write a JSON file with summary and tasks, each containing id_ref, title, body (inputs, deliverables, acceptance), assigned_to (supplied Worker ID), optional depends_on_refs. Use `{{ .CLICommand }} task plan --task <parent> --plan-file <path>`. Planning saves tasks; it never dispatches them.
- Keep follow-up work for the same goal under its original unfinished parent. When testing finds defects, review the submitted test report as completed work (accepting a truthful defect report does not mean the product passed). Append BOTH a developer repair task depending on that test task ID and a regression test task depending on the new repair id_ref using `{{ .CLICommand }} task plan --task <parent> --append --request-id <stable-feedback-id> --plan-file <path>`. The JSON includes only new children; depends_on_refs may name existing sibling task IDs or new refs in this batch. Reuse the request ID and exact payload on retries. Do not submit another parent, overwrite completed work, or close the current parent to unblock the room queue. Dispatch the repair, wait for its result and accept it before dispatching regression testing. Repeat within this parent if regression finds more defects; if progress stalls, explain the blocker and wait instead of retrying indefinitely.
- Explicitly start an eligible child with `{{ .CLICommand }} task dispatch --task <child>`. Dependencies require Manager acceptance. Multiple parents queue; only the oldest unfinished parent runs. Different rooms remain independent. If blocked, explain once and end the turn. After dispatch, end the turn and wait for a new message.
- A QA task is completed when its requested testing and truthful report are delivered, even if tests reveal product defects. Use completed with failing checks and reproduction evidence in the result; failed/blocked mean the assigned work itself could not be completed. Manager acceptance of the report enables a repair task, not a claim that the product passed.
- As Worker, use the supplied task and predecessor deliverables; do not list other work. Claim before executing: `{{ .CLICommand }} task claim --task <task_id> --actor-id <participant_id> --attempt <attempt>`. Claim failure means stop, not untracked execution. Submit with `{{ .CLICommand }} task update --task <task_id> --actor-id <participant_id> --attempt <attempt> --status <completed|failed|blocked> --result <deliverables>` (use --error or --reason for failure/blockage). completed means pending Manager review. The server records the result and notifies Manager; end the turn without polling or notifying another Worker.
- As Manager, review submitted work using the exact attempt: `{{ .CLICommand }} task review --task <child> --attempt <attempt> --accept --result <assessment>` (omit --accept to request corrections). Then explicitly dispatch eligible children. Dependencies never advance automatically. Once children are accepted, publish `{{ .CLICommand }} task report --task <parent> --outcome <succeeded|issues|failed|stopped> --result <assessment>`.
- Report succeeded only after the goal and required regression tests pass. Report issues only for an explicitly concluded partial delivery with no outstanding child work; it is not a way to move ongoing repairs into a new parent. Failed/stopped are explicit terminal decisions, not routine responses to a QA defect. Publish deliverables, findings and final explanations in chat; the task popup is only a status checklist. The server attaches the task reference only to the first plan message, so do not repeat task cards in later replies.
- For a needed question or relay use `{{ .CLICommand }} task message --task <id> --actor-id <participant_id> --target <member_id> --message-id <stable_id> --body <text>`. All Worker mentions route through Manager; Workers do not self-assign or delegate.
- Never create Teams, direct-agent tasks, another room or private-chat dispatch for this work. Do not invent or add assignees. Do not bypass runtime scope checks with HTTP. Delivery failures use `{{ .CLICommand }} task retry-delivery --room <room>` without re-executing work. Internal context and CLI instructions are private; chat contains human-readable coordination and results.

### Scope

- Use direct `csgclaw-cli` commands for routine room, participant, member, and message operations after workers already exist.
- This includes creating rooms, listing rooms or participants, listing room members, adding room members, and sending messages including structured mentions.
- Do not use this rule set to create a new worker. For new agent provisioning or hub-template creation, use the dedicated agent/template creation flow instead of bare participant-create commands.
- Do not use this rule set for multi-worker orchestration, cross-worker sequencing, or tracker-style task coordination; use `agent-teams` when team execution is intended.

### Workflow

1. Identify the exact room, participant, member, or message operation needed.
2. If room context matters, inspect it first with `room list` or `member list`, especially to determine whether the room is direct.
3. Check the current CLI surface with `{{ .CLICommand }} <entity> -h` or `{{ .CLICommand }} <entity> <verb> -h` before assuming flags.
4. Execute the smallest direct CLI command that completes the request.
5. Report the concrete result, such as the room ID, participant ID, member summary, or sent message result.

### Room And Participant Rules

- Use participant IDs at the CLI boundary. For the local CSGClaw manager use `manager`; use `u-manager` only for agent routes or API fields that require an agent ID.
- Run `participant list` before creating or mentioning a worker if the user may be referring to an existing participant.
- Verify room membership with `member list` when room presence matters, and after adding a member when membership is part of the task.
- A direct room cannot accept an added participant as a new member. Create a new room with the complete `--member-ids` set that includes the current DM participants and the new participant instead.
- Keep channel-specific IDs straight. In the local `csgclaw` channel use CSGClaw participant IDs; in Feishu use the configured Feishu participant IDs.

### Worker Notification Rules

- Workers are `mention_only`: they react to structured mentions, not plain-text `@name`.
- Notify workers with `{{ .CLICommand }} message create --mention-id <participant-id>` and verify delivery with `message list`.
- Confirm that the stored message contains a structured `<at user_id="...">` tag; do not assume prose like `@worker-name` wakes the worker.
- Before first dispatch, run `participant list` to resolve the worker participant ID and `member list` to confirm the worker is in the room.

### Operating Rules

- Prefer direct `csgclaw-cli` commands over ad hoc HTTP calls.
- Keep the response focused on the concrete CLI result instead of introducing unrelated planning artifacts.
- Use `agent-teams` for multi-worker team orchestration; use manual room messaging only when direct dispatch is actually intended.

